1
00:00:00,780 --> 00:00:07,580
Congratulations! You've passed a one percent treaty. Now you've got twenty-seven point two billion dollars per year flowing

2
00:00:07,580 --> 00:00:13,360
into the one percent Treaty Fund. That's the good news. The bad news is that humans are involved.

3
00:00:14,240 --> 00:00:21,680
Not all of it is available for allocation. First, the automatic deductions (think of these as the "cost of convincing humans

4
00:00:21,680 --> 00:00:29,480
to stop killing each other" tax): ten percent goes to VICTORY Bond investors, another ten percent goes to political incentives.

5
00:00:30,080 --> 00:00:35,540
That leaves eighty percent (twenty-one point eight billion dollars) for Wishocracy to allocate.

6
00:00:36,100 --> 00:00:41,560
How do you stop parasitic special interests from stealing that twenty-one point eight billion dollars?

7
00:00:42,020 --> 00:00:49,200
If history is any guide, you don't. Every pool of money humanity has ever created has attracted lobbyists the way a dropped

8
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:52,920
ice cream cone attracts ants, except ants at least work hard.

9
00:00:53,820 --> 00:00:58,060
Wishocracy replaces committees with code and representatives with mathematics.

10
00:00:58,660 --> 00:01:05,200
It lets eight billion people collectively decide how to spend twenty-one point eight billion dollars without turning it

11
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:09,600
into a bureaucratic nightmare. The difference between this and the N.I.H.

12
00:01:09,600 --> 00:01:14,720
is like asking 200 bureaucrats what you should have for lunch versus asking your stomach.

13
00:01:15,400 --> 00:01:19,340
Your stomach has never once funded a colleague's lunch out of professional courtesy.

14
00:01:20,340 --> 00:01:24,700
How Wishocracy Allocates the one percent Treaty Fund: Decentralized Crowdfunding.

15
00:01:26,160 --> 00:01:32,620
Your decentralized institutes of health, or D.I.H. network functions as a decentralized crowdfunding platform.

16
00:01:33,520 --> 00:01:40,540
Anyone can submit a campaign proposal. Wishocracy allocates funds from the one percent Treaty Fund across competing campaigns.

17
00:01:41,080 --> 00:01:45,680
It's Kickstarter, but backed by the G.D.P. of nations instead of your friend's credit card.

18
00:01:46,560 --> 00:01:50,360
What the decentralized framework for drug assessment, or D.F.D.A.

19
00:01:50,680 --> 00:01:59,420
(a decentralized FDA) handles automatically (no voting required): Which treatments get tested: Companies register, patients

20
00:01:59,420 --> 00:02:07,400
choose (capitalism, for once, doing something useful). Which diseases get researched: Patients join trials for their conditions

21
00:02:07,400 --> 00:02:15,060
(people tend to be motivated about their own mortality). Resource allocation within trials: Market prices and participant

22
00:02:15,060 --> 00:02:23,840
choices. What Wishocracy Actually Decides: The hard stuff. Allocation across campaign proposals competing for one percent

23
00:02:23,840 --> 00:02:28,600
Treaty Fund funds: Infrastructure Campaigns (the boring but essential plumbing).

24
00:02:29,300 --> 00:02:35,680
Proposals for building and maintaining the platform itself: drug assessment framework development, electronic health record

25
00:02:35,680 --> 00:02:41,380
integrations, cloud infrastructure, security audits, and competing alternative implementations.

26
00:02:42,200 --> 00:02:46,840
Budgets range from a few million a year for support services to a billion for the core platform.

27
00:02:48,280 --> 00:02:54,000
Public Goods (things the market won't fund because nobody gets rich curing diseases that don't sell pills).

28
00:02:54,860 --> 00:02:59,800
The big-ticket items. Patient trial subsidies. Off-patent drug research.

29
00:02:59,820 --> 00:03:07,280
Rare disease initiatives. And a negative results publishing fund, because knowing what doesn't work is worth billions; your

30
00:03:07,280 --> 00:03:11,620
species keeps re-running failed experiments because nobody publishes failures.

31
00:03:12,340 --> 00:03:18,240
These programs absorb the bulk of the budget. Service Provider Bids (capitalism, but supervised).

32
00:03:19,220 --> 00:03:25,660
Competing providers bid for contracts: data storage, clinical trial insurance pools, and other operational needs.

33
00:03:25,780 --> 00:03:33,380
Multiple providers compete on price and quality. How It Works: Pairwise Comparisons Between Campaigns.

34
00:03:33,400 --> 00:03:38,780
No committee on Earth has ever looked at a list of ten thousand proposals and made a good decision.

35
00:03:39,360 --> 00:03:47,560
Committees are where good ideas go to die of old age. Instead, the global population votes using pairwise comparisons: "What's

36
00:03:47,560 --> 00:03:52,640
more important right now:" "Framework for drug assessment development" vs "Alzheimer's prize"?

37
00:03:53,660 --> 00:04:00,540
"Epic integration" vs "Security audits"? "Rare disease research" vs "An alternative drug assessment framework"?

38
00:04:01,340 --> 00:04:07,140
"Patient subsidies" vs "Infrastructure spending"? Millions of people make simple pairwise choices.

39
00:04:08,020 --> 00:04:10,960
The algorithm aggregates preferences into funding allocations.

40
00:04:11,580 --> 00:04:18,339
No filibustering. No horse-trading. No senators holding medical research hostage because they want a highway in their district.

41
00:04:19,440 --> 00:04:24,740
Why You Can't Just Use a Spreadsheet. Someone is going to ask, "Why not just write a formula?"

42
00:04:25,200 --> 00:04:32,460
Because the decisions look like this: Multiple competing implementations - Three teams want to build the drug assessment

43
00:04:32,460 --> 00:04:37,260
platform. Which one? All three? Two? The one with the best logo?

44
00:04:38,240 --> 00:04:45,900
Trade-offs between prizes - one hundred billion dollars for aging reversal or fifty billion dollars for Alzheimer's plus

45
00:04:45,900 --> 00:04:50,320
fifty billion dollars for cancer? There is no "correct" answer.

46
00:04:50,620 --> 00:04:55,800
There is only what humanity collectively values, which is exactly what Wishocracy measures.

47
00:04:57,100 --> 00:05:01,660
Public goods allocation - How much for off-patent drugs vs rare diseases?

48
00:05:02,420 --> 00:05:09,000
Markets won't answer this because there's no profit in it. Service provider competition - Which companies get contracts

49
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:14,300
for what amounts? Your decentralized framework for drug assessment (D.F.D.A.

50
00:05:14,960 --> 00:05:21,720
(a decentralized FDA)) allocates resources within pragmatic clinical trials.

51
00:05:21,800 --> 00:05:27,480
Wishocracy allocates resources between campaigns. One is the engine, the other is the steering wheel.

52
00:05:28,020 --> 00:05:36,540
You need both unless you enjoy driving into walls. The core of Wishocracy is a voting method so simple, even a congressman

53
00:05:36,540 --> 00:05:44,140
could do it (though we'd recommend they practice first). It's called Aggregated Pairwise Preference Allocation, or simply,

54
00:05:44,300 --> 00:05:49,540
the Pairwise Slider Allocation. Your brain can't rank a list of twenty priorities.

55
00:05:50,020 --> 00:05:54,740
It gives up around item number seven. But it's fantastic at comparing two things.

56
00:05:55,680 --> 00:06:03,280
Instead of asking, "Rank these ten thousand campaign proposals," the system asks: "What's more important right now: D.F.D.A.

57
00:06:03,460 --> 00:06:07,640
(a decentralized FDA) platform development or security audits?"

58
00:06:08,300 --> 00:06:12,660
You make a choice. That's it. You do this a few times with different random pairs.

59
00:06:13,220 --> 00:06:19,460
It takes five minutes. Less time than you spend choosing a Netflix show, and considerably more important.

60
00:06:19,600 --> 00:06:26,680
Millions of other people do the same. An algorithm takes all these simple, head-to-head comparisons and builds a real-time

61
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:30,540
ranking of infrastructure and public goods priorities. (Remember: Wishocracy doesn't allocate between diseases.

62
00:06:30,540 --> 00:06:30,540
Patients do that by choosing which trials to join. Wishocracy allocates between infrastructure campaigns and public goods

63
00:06:30,540 --> 00:06:34,000
that markets can't handle.). It's the wisdom of crowds, but without the crowds having to talk to each other.

64
00:06:35,040 --> 00:06:42,780
Why This Actually Works (Math Warning). When millions of people make pairwise choices, something almost magical happens

65
00:06:43,300 --> 00:06:50,260
(and by "magical" I mean "statistically inevitable," which is the only kind of magic that actually works): Random pairs

66
00:06:50,260 --> 00:06:54,930
prevent gaming. You can't lobby 5 million people. Well, you can try.

67
00:06:55,300 --> 00:07:02,840
Good luck. Statistical models (Bradley-Terry, PageRank) extract global preferences from individual comparisons.

68
00:07:03,340 --> 00:07:08,060
The same math that ranks Google search results can rank humanity's medical priorities.

69
00:07:09,080 --> 00:07:12,560
Google uses it to find cat videos. You'll use it to cure cancer.

70
00:07:13,380 --> 00:07:21,140
Progress. Outliers cancel out. The guy who votes "fund my personal jetpack" gets drowned out by the millions voting for

71
00:07:21,140 --> 00:07:26,400
"cure my mother's Alzheimer's.". Wisdom of crowds emerges. Reliably.

72
00:07:26,980 --> 00:07:32,780
Repeatedly. Boringly. Example: 5 million people vote. Each makes 20 comparisons.

73
00:07:33,580 --> 00:07:40,700
The algorithm processes all 100 million data points in seconds and produces a budget: patient subsidies get the biggest

74
00:07:40,700 --> 00:07:48,560
share, followed by platform development, off-patent drug research, rare diseases, and smaller slices for security and negative

75
00:07:48,560 --> 00:07:52,680
results publishing. No committee meetings were harmed in the making of this budget.

76
00:07:53,440 --> 00:08:00,180
It's democracy without the shouting. The stupidity is still there, but it's evenly distributed across millions of people

77
00:08:00,180 --> 00:08:03,940
and thus cancels out. This is called "the wisdom of crowds."

78
00:08:04,880 --> 00:08:10,740
It works for the same reason that guessing the weight of a cow at a fair works: individually you're all terrible at it,

79
00:08:11,060 --> 00:08:17,280
but collectively you're weirdly accurate. From Priorities to Projects (Where Wishes Become Tasks).

80
00:08:18,420 --> 00:08:24,280
Great, so the Pairwise Slider Allocation tells you "Curing Alzheimer's" is a top priority.

81
00:08:24,980 --> 00:08:29,100
Wonderful. Now what? Having a priority without a plan is just a wish.

82
00:08:29,680 --> 00:08:35,700
And wishes, as a rule, don't cure anything. Wishocracy translates that priority into action.

83
00:08:36,600 --> 00:08:44,400
A.I. Breaks It Down: An A.I. takes the impossible goal of "Cure Alzheimer's" and breaks it into thousands of smaller, concrete,

84
00:08:44,500 --> 00:08:48,920
fundable tasks. "Cure Alzheimer's" becomes "Map protein structures."

85
00:08:49,800 --> 00:08:54,260
That becomes "Run AlphaFold on these sequences." That becomes "Rent computing time."

86
00:08:55,340 --> 00:08:59,920
Every impossible problem is just a series of possible steps arranged in a line.

87
00:09:00,500 --> 00:09:06,520
Your species figured this out for building pyramids 4,500 years ago but keeps forgetting to apply it to medicine.

88
00:09:07,440 --> 00:09:11,840
The Bounty Board: The system posts these tasks to a global marketplace.

89
00:09:12,220 --> 00:09:16,680
It's like eBay, but for saving humanity. WANTED: A cure for Alzheimer's.

90
00:09:17,420 --> 00:09:22,820
BOUNTY: ten billion dollars. WANTED: A map of all protein misfolding patterns.

91
00:09:23,480 --> 00:09:30,720
BOUNTY: five hundred million dollars. The World Competes: The best teams from around the world, from M.I.T.

92
00:09:30,940 --> 00:09:37,500
to some kid in a garage in Lagos, bid on these tasks. The system funds multiple approaches in parallel.

93
00:09:38,120 --> 00:09:42,490
The ones that show promise get more funding. The ones that fail lose funding instantly.

94
00:09:43,500 --> 00:09:51,220
It's venture capital, but for not dying. Your species already uses this method to decide which restaurants survive and which

95
00:09:51,220 --> 00:09:55,380
phone apps get downloaded. Time to try it with the thing that kills you.
